<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 10 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">Japan from Jōmon to Meiji</h1><p class="path-tagline">Follow the Japanese archipelago from its first potters to the emperor's restored rule.</p><p class="path-intro">For some 14,000 years the people of the Japanese archipelago lived mainly by hunting, gathering and fishing, and their potters made the world's oldest known ceramics. Wet-rice farming came with the Yayoi, and the Yamato court around Nara extended its authority over much of the islands. From the late twelfth century warrior governments held power: the Kamakura and Ashikaga shogunates, then, after the unification begun by Oda Nobunaga, the Tokugawa shoguns at Edo. This path follows that story to 1868, when the Meiji Restoration returned power to the emperor and set Japan on the way to becoming an industrial great power.</p><p class="path-types">Suits <a href="/start">the Chronicler</a> · <a href="/start">the Strategist</a></p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/culture/jomon-culture"><span class="path-step-ph">1</span><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Profile · c. 14000 BCE – c. 300 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Jōmon culture</span><span class="path-step-why">For some 14,000 years hunters, gatherers and fishers lived in settled villages here, and their potters made the world's oldest known ceramics, around 14,500 BCE.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/culture/yayoi-culture"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wet-rice-agriculture.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Profile · c. 300 BCE – c. 300 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Yayoi culture</span><span class="path-step-why">Farming migrants from the Korean Peninsula and the continent brought wet-rice farming, bronze and iron, and mixed with the Jōmon population from about 300 BCE.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/yamato-court"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yamato-court.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Encyclopedia · State · c. 250–710</span><span class="path-step-name">Yamato court</span><span class="path-step-why">Centred around modern Nara, the court from which the imperial line descends built keyhole-shaped burial mounds and received writing and Buddhism through Baekje.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/heian-kyo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/heian-kyo.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Encyclopedia · Place</span><span class="path-step-name">Heian-kyō</span><span class="path-step-why">Founded by Emperor Kanmu in 794 on a grid modelled on Chang'an, the capital on the site of modern Kyoto remained the imperial seat for more than a thousand years.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/minamoto-no-yoritomo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/minamoto-no-yoritomo.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Encyclopedia · Person · 1147–1199</span><span class="path-step-name">Minamoto no Yoritomo</span><span class="path-step-why">Victor over the Taira in the Genpei War of 1180–1185, Yoritomo set up a warrior government at Kamakura and received the title of shogun in 1192.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-takauji"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ashikaga-takauji.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Encyclopedia · Person · 1305–1358</span><span class="path-step-name">Ashikaga Takauji</span><span class="path-step-why">Turning against the Kamakura shogunate in 1333, Takauji took Kyoto in 1336 and in 1338 became shogun, founding the Ashikaga, or Muromachi, shogunate.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">7 · Encyclopedia · Person · 1534–1582</span><span class="path-step-name">Oda Nobunaga</span><span class="path-step-why">A warlord of the Sengoku period, Nobunaga began the military unification of Japan that Toyotomi Hideyoshi continued and Tokugawa Ieyasu completed.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/tokugawa-shogunate"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edo-castle.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">8 · Profile · 1603 CE – 1868 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Tokugawa Shogunate</span><span class="path-step-why">After victory at Sekigahara the Tokugawa shoguns ruled from Edo, keeping some 260 daimyo in check through two and a half centuries of internal peace.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/movement/meiji-restoration"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">9 · Profile · 1853 CE – 1889 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Meiji Restoration</span><span class="path-step-why">Perry's expedition and the unequal treaties undermined the shogunate; in 1868 Emperor Meiji declared power restored, and the domains and the samurai class were abolished.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/empire-of-japan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-meiji.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">10 · Profile · 1868 CE – 1945 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Empire of Japan</span><span class="path-step-why">Born of the Restoration, Japan went from feudal isolation to industrial great power in barely a generation, until a war it could not sustain ended in surrender in 1945.</span></span></a></li></ol>
